# Citation metadata for the OpenSpecLib software and the OpenSpecLib Format specification.
# Release checklist (docs/publishing.md): update `version` and add `date-released`
# when a release is tagged. Add the Zenodo concept DOI under `identifiers` once the
# first release has been archived.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use OpenSpecLib or the OpenSpecLib Format, please cite it using the
  metadata below. The spectra themselves come from their source libraries,
  each with its own licence and citation: cite those sources too (see each
  collection file's footer metadata, or docs/provenance.md).
type: software
title: "OpenSpecLib: an open format and toolkit for spectral libraries"
abstract: >-
  OpenSpecLib is a Python toolkit that ingests public spectral libraries
  (USGS Spectral Library Version 7, ECOSTRESS, EcoSIS and others), validates
  them, and publishes them in the OpenSpecLib Format: one self-describing
  Apache Parquet file per collection, with inline wavelength axes, required
  quantity and measurement geometry, NaN for missing values, and SPDX
  licences. The repository holds the format specification, a conformance
  checker, converters and a web viewer.
authors:
  - family-names: Jones
    given-names: Ellie
version: 0.2.0
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/null-jones/openspeclib"
url: "https://null-jones.github.io/openspeclib/"
keywords:
  - spectral library
  - spectroscopy
  - reflectance
  - remote sensing
  - hyperspectral
  - Apache Parquet
  - data standard
references:
  - type: standard
    title: "OpenSpecLib Format"
    version: 1.0.0
    authors:
      - family-names: Jones
        given-names: Ellie
    url: "https://github.com/null-jones/openspeclib/blob/v0.2.0/docs/spec/format-v1.md"
    repository-code: "https://github.com/null-jones/openspeclib"
    license: MIT
    notes: >-
      Specification version 1.0.0. The specification is versioned
      independently of the openspeclib package and of data releases.
